00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
i'm joined now by the director of the  oxford vaccine group and chairperson of  the joint committee on vaccination and  immunization professor andrew pollard uh  great to talk to you today uh sir andrew  it's been an extraordinary 12 months  hasn't it in the life of both covert and  the astrazeneca vaccine what have we  learned  well i think it as you say it's been a  quite extraordinary year that we've just  been through from at this time last year  when we were just starting roll out of  vaccines  with a lot of uncertainty still about  how long it would take to start to bring  the pandemic under control but i think  on on that day  last year i certainly felt that this  that was the beginning of the end of the  pandemic uh clearly as the years gone on  um we've seen the most remarkable  rollout of vaccines not just the oxford  vaccine of all of them around the world  with  now  around nine billion doses  that have been distributed and the most  incredible impact on  the the pandemic  so that we're in a very much better  place today than we were we were  today with very large numbers of cases  in the community um but still  very good control of severe disease  as a result of vaccination they've been  extraordinary  are you worried that we're becoming a  little complacent  um no i don't think we are being  complacent  at all actually i mean we're  today in a situation where there's very  good monitoring much better than it was  before globally  trying to understand  uh how best to make sure  everyone in the world is protected um as  well as is possible and now we're in  this new period of transition to  work out now that there's very high  levels of immunity in countries like  ours how do we transition to a situation  where society is open  and we maintain protection of the  vulnerable in the population  and that is the the programs that have  been happening over the last few months  with the doses being given and trying to  manage the number of cases so the nhs is  protected um but as we go forwards into  the year ahead uh working out better how  to live with the virus um is going to be  the critical next step  you mentioned third dose well of course  countries like israel they're already  doing false doses do you foresee a  position where we are going to be pretty  much getting jabbed every six months or  so  well it's just not from a global  perspective affordable sustainable or  deliverable to give forth doses to  everyone on the planet every every six  months and remember that today  less than 10 percent of people in  low-income countries have been even had  their first dose and so the whole idea  of regular fourth doses globally is just  not um  sensible  now it may be that  as the science evolves that we can work  out who the most vulnerable are in  populations and target future boosters  to those individuals to maintain their  protection but for the vast majority of  people  who are vaccinated the risk now is  extremely low of severe coverage of  those who've had three differences and  it's likely that we'll reach a point  where we're focusing those booster doses  on those who most need them and of  course at this moment we don't know what  that looks like does that mean that we  need updated vaccines each year like we  do with flu we just use the same  vaccines for boosters we need more data  to make those decision decisions  and in terms of those who haven't had  vaccinations um there has been a lot of  misinformation out there how frustrating  is it for you  as a scientist who's been hugely  involved in in the science of  vaccination all your life um  to see some of the the stuff that is  repeated usually online and what impact  do you think this has had has it  actually  caused people to die who might not have  died  well there's no doubt that anything  which um  makes people uncertain or hesitant about  being vaccinated um particularly  those who are in vulnerable groups  that puts their life at risk and we've  definitely  seen the effects of  some parts of the commentary some of the  misinformation which have had a major  impact on  people's confidence in vaccination and  so there's no doubt in my mind that  during the course of the last year some  people will have died because of  misinformation and that just shows how  critical it is it's another type of  virus that spreads very readily around  the world  is there anything you would have done  differently now looking back over the  last 12 years because yes you've been  involved in vaccines all this time but  it's been unlike anything else in terms  of the scale  um well i i mean our  major role in uh 2020 was in vaccine  development and i think the one thing um  that i would have liked to have been  able to do differently uh would be to  run much larger clinical trials and we  couldn't do that  partly because  the human resource is required to do  that that we needed at the time  but largely just we didn't have the  manufacturing capacity to make enough  doses to run larger trials  the reason why that's so important is if  we could have done that we may have had  a vaccine a month or two earlier and  with 10 000 people dying every day at  the end of 2020  it really shows you if you can just make  a few weeks difference  to shave off the time it takes to  develop actions you can save many lives  around the world and so i think that for  me is the the biggest thing that if we  were starting this again that would be  to find ways of actually having more  doses to run larger trials and get an  answer quicker  and during the course of uh the the last  year  um i don't think um  that we have to we should shy away from  the problem that we've had from a global  community perspective  which has been timely vaccination of  vulnerable people everywhere millions of  people have died during 2021 because  they weren't vaccinated um in before  they got disease and many of those  people died because they didn't have  access to vaccines so the equitable  distribution of vaccines the other thing  which i think  we really need to think about how to  address not just for future pandemics  but even today in this pandemic to make  sure and the people are protected  everywhere  in terms of new variants because we have  a vaccine as it were for covert  generally  do we need to be less concerned about  new variants or should we be as equally  concerned i mean it does appear that  omicron is possibly not as serious for  lots of people as delta was but there  could be another one just around the  corner tomorrow couldn't there  well it would be great if uh if we're in  a situation now where the cost to the  virus of being able to spread now that  was we're vaccinated is that it causes  less severe disease that would be good  i don't think we can be sure at this  moment that future versions of the  coronavirus that the sons and daughters  of omicron  will  be uh  mild  causing mild disease so i think we do  need more time to evaluate that to  understand exactly why omicron seems to  be causing milder disease is this a  direction of travel that the virus has  to go in in order to survive in  continuing  infections  if in indeed we do have ongoing problems  with more severe disease updated  vaccines for the new variants may be one  of the ways that we manage  the living with the virus in the future  do you feel instinctively that we are  over the worst of omicron perhaps uh i  don't want to be overly optimistic or  put words in your mouth  well i don't know with omicron at the  moment i think as the modelers have been  saying in the media over the last few  days one of the real difficulties during  this period is the type of mixing  patterns of people over the holidays  makes it very difficult to  fully assess where we are and i think  that's going to become unclear in the  the weeks ahead  i i don't think we're completely  overwhelmed mikron certainly i mean the  uk but even less so in many other  countries um and there's still some  weeks for the fall of that to play out  but  generally the news is relatively good um  from an overall perspective in that it  does seem to be causing milder disease  but that doesn't mean that our health  system  which is  very much on the edge in normal times is  going to be able to cope with the the  increase in cases and so i think we do  still have to have measures in place  that protect and the health service  during this winter  and what about can i ask you about the  the vaccine manufacturing and innovation  center uh this is a facility that that  creates and tests new vaccines  government's looking at privatizing that  is that something you would welcome is  that useful or does that fill you with  dismay  well i think it's really important to  have access to  the manufacturing facilities that  provide more opportunity for producing  vaccines between pandemics and in  pandemics so that we're much better  prepared  for the future now how that will work  from an operational perspective uh  whether it's privatized or not i don't  know um and that's i think this it sort  of brings some uncertainty for us at  this moment about what the operating  model is  so i think you know we need to wait and  see what that says  uncertainty is never especially useful  is it uh professor andrew pollard  director of the oxford vaccine group  great to talk to you today uh what an  achievement and we're just 12 months  down the line from it thanks so much for  talking to us thank you

